1
00:00:00,041 --> 00:00:05,574
Everyone, I'm Ishan Shah, and I'm here
to talk about how can feature flags

2
00:00:05,772 --> 00:00:08,243
be helpful in an incident evidence.

3
00:00:09,659 --> 00:00:13,291
A feature flag got changed, and
we see a latency in checkout.

4
00:00:13,887 --> 00:00:17,343
An AI agent would recommend, "Let's
just go ahead and roll back."

5
00:00:18,114 --> 00:00:19,015
Do we approve it?

6
00:00:20,291 --> 00:00:21,053
Not yet, right?

7
00:00:21,635 --> 00:00:25,382
Timing gives us a hypothesis,
not a permission to just go

8
00:00:25,384 --> 00:00:27,082
ahead and act as required.

9
00:00:28,113 --> 00:00:31,435
Today, I'll show how feature
flags can become reliable incident

10
00:00:31,437 --> 00:00:35,715
evidence while policy and human
judgment still remain in control.

11
00:00:35,988 --> 00:00:36,744
Human in the loop.

12
00:00:37,798 --> 00:00:40,640
Beacon by itself is
broader than feature flags.

13
00:00:40,882 --> 00:00:44,827
It is an evidence-first incident
reasoning workflow that connects

14
00:00:45,013 --> 00:00:48,087
telemetry, Kubernetes, and
deployment state, repository

15
00:00:48,087 --> 00:00:52,119
history, service context, operator
decisions, and remediation handoff.

16
00:00:53,019 --> 00:00:56,879
For this talk, I am zooming in on
one part inside that larger system.

17
00:00:57,572 --> 00:01:00,753
Where should feature flags enter
the incident workflow, and what

18
00:01:00,813 --> 00:01:02,507
authority should they have?

19
00:01:03,105 --> 00:01:05,191
Here is a problem behind that decision.

20
00:01:06,096 --> 00:01:09,942
Imagine checkout latency rising
after several nearby changes.

21
00:01:10,677 --> 00:01:14,815
A deployment lands, the tax V2
lookup reaches full rollout,

22
00:01:15,234 --> 00:01:18,846
database pressure changes, and a
downstream dependency becomes noisy.

23
00:01:19,684 --> 00:01:24,723
The nearest change that is to
roll back is tempting, but is

24
00:01:24,723 --> 00:01:26,103
not automatically the cause.

25
00:01:26,822 --> 00:01:30,825
What follows is a design position
backed by a small prototype.

26
00:01:31,509 --> 00:01:34,604
The prototype implements a smaller
version of the full design.

27
00:01:35,479 --> 00:01:41,809
An evidence-first agent should reserve
all the four explanations instead of

28
00:01:41,825 --> 00:01:43,477
jumping to the most convenient one.

29
00:01:44,568 --> 00:01:46,707
A timeline narrows the search.

30
00:01:46,866 --> 00:01:52,631
It doesn't still prove the cause
for the failure So, there are four

31
00:01:52,631 --> 00:01:56,967
different hypotheses, four different
evidence paths that we consider.

32
00:01:58,556 --> 00:02:01,119
Each hypothesis needs a
different evidence path.

33
00:02:01,821 --> 00:02:05,553
A deployment regression needs
repository history, Kubernetes

34
00:02:05,571 --> 00:02:07,378
rollout state, and pod behavior.

35
00:02:08,366 --> 00:02:12,786
A feature flag hypothesis needs
the provider audit event, targeting

36
00:02:12,810 --> 00:02:14,989
rule, and evaluated cohort.

37
00:02:16,150 --> 00:02:19,981
Database saturation needs connection
pool and resource metrics.

38
00:02:20,815 --> 00:02:24,893
And a tag service hypothesis
needs dependency latency, errors,

39
00:02:24,991 --> 00:02:27,511
retry behavior, everything
that we know about the service.

40
00:02:28,271 --> 00:02:32,964
The agent may rank this hypothesis
to find what the cause is, but

41
00:02:32,964 --> 00:02:37,828
ranking cannot replace the evidence
needed to rule each one in or out.

42
00:02:38,737 --> 00:02:43,358
This avoids a common failure mode
where every nearby change is forced

43
00:02:43,383 --> 00:02:45,518
into the same generic telemetry story.

44
00:02:46,589 --> 00:02:47,072
Rollback.

45
00:02:47,636 --> 00:02:53,160
For root cause analysis, for finding
out whether a flag event is on or

46
00:02:53,178 --> 00:02:57,462
off, yes, it tells us that we enabled
something which caused the failure,

47
00:02:57,773 --> 00:03:00,251
and it helps us roll back immediately.

48
00:03:01,099 --> 00:03:04,111
But just relying on on
or off might be too weak.

49
00:03:04,905 --> 00:03:09,118
We need to preserve the transition
timestamp environment, the actor

50
00:03:09,126 --> 00:03:13,301
acting on it, targeting rule,
cohort, reason, and source event.

51
00:03:14,148 --> 00:03:19,041
Keep the observed outcome as separate
telemetry evidence, so the flag record

52
00:03:19,196 --> 00:03:22,697
does not smuggle into a cause conclusion.

53
00:03:23,674 --> 00:03:27,774
Beacon currently has typed evidence
and evaluated flag context.

54
00:03:28,260 --> 00:03:33,122
The richer audit history fields show
here are the production design target,

55
00:03:33,492 --> 00:03:35,936
not a claim about the current adapter.

56
00:03:37,112 --> 00:03:45,998
So what a flag cannot tell us A
provider audit event has hard limits.

57
00:03:46,170 --> 00:03:50,276
Its timestamp is not necessarily the
moment every SDK picked up the change.

58
00:03:51,208 --> 00:03:55,507
Polling, streaming, caches, and client
state can all create propagation lag.

59
00:03:56,577 --> 00:04:00,594
A 10% rollout does not identify
the cohort unless targeting and

60
00:04:00,614 --> 00:04:02,582
evaluation contexts are preserved.

61
00:04:03,383 --> 00:04:05,936
Other flags may have also
changed in the same window.

62
00:04:06,655 --> 00:04:11,042
Most importantly, a flag cannot--
can change without the affected

63
00:04:11,071 --> 00:04:12,941
code path ever being evaluated.

64
00:04:14,457 --> 00:04:19,572
A good root cause analysis keeps the
audit event, evaluation evidence, and

65
00:04:19,611 --> 00:04:21,635
observed behavior as separate records.

66
00:04:22,475 --> 00:04:28,306
A flag event is not proof that the traffic
evaluated the flag pa- flagged path.

67
00:04:28,845 --> 00:04:32,456
So let's see, like we've, we've been
talking about the timeline changes, right?

68
00:04:33,015 --> 00:04:38,134
So when you talk about an agent into
this, the incident timeline should

69
00:04:38,171 --> 00:04:40,341
combine type changes from every source.

70
00:04:41,102 --> 00:04:42,527
What were the deployment events?

71
00:04:42,631 --> 00:04:43,966
What were the flag transitions?

72
00:04:44,238 --> 00:04:45,986
What are the configuration changes?

73
00:04:46,253 --> 00:04:47,911
Were there any scaling events?

74
00:04:48,356 --> 00:04:49,683
And what was the first symptom?

75
00:04:50,858 --> 00:04:54,586
Notice that the configuration and
scaling events occur after the flag

76
00:04:54,589 --> 00:04:56,385
