00:15:35,738 --> 00:15:38,803
as well as PagerDuty, as well
as on the Beacon prototype.

230
00:15:40,591 --> 00:15:46,616
Now, let's think of a scenario where
we have something which is crucial or

231
00:15:46,618 --> 00:15:52,635
critical data like PII data or a service
for which we are doing chaos testing, or

232
00:15:52,663 --> 00:15:57,099
we are running some synthetic data, and we
don't want it to be processed by a model.

233
00:15:57,582 --> 00:16:01,089
Because when we generate the ranked
hypothesis, it is through a model.

234
00:16:01,949 --> 00:16:06,055
So how can I exclude certain
incidents to be not even

235
00:16:06,113 --> 00:16:08,279
considered to be sent to a model?

236
00:16:09,006 --> 00:16:10,407
And that comes through governance.

237
00:16:11,323 --> 00:16:16,409
So if you see in this incident, it
matched some of the rules that we had,

238
00:16:17,228 --> 00:16:21,982
we had enabled, and it says that RCA
gets rejected, the incident stays gated.

239
00:16:22,526 --> 00:16:29,981
So in this scenario, the-- nothing
is sent from the beacon to any model.

240
00:16:30,894 --> 00:16:36,753
So the incident explicitly stays gated
because it is not allowed to operate on

241
00:16:36,754 --> 00:16:42,101
this incident So this has some of the
different governance rules, and it's,

242
00:16:42,103 --> 00:16:47,786
basically a free config where you can say
that any low severity alerts should be

243
00:16:47,786 --> 00:16:52,837
skipped, any services which are sandboxed
should be skipped, any data that contains

244
00:16:52,842 --> 00:16:55,067
something synthetic can be skipped.

245
00:16:56,409 --> 00:17:00,687
Now, one thing where I've been burned
in the past is that an agent does

246
00:17:00,687 --> 00:17:04,815
something and it says, "Oh sorry, it
cannot roll back because it has no audit

247
00:17:04,819 --> 00:17:06,408
trail or history it can go back to."

248
00:17:07,185 --> 00:17:12,331
So when we talk about incident
management agent, we want each and every

249
00:17:12,335 --> 00:17:17,614
decision taken as part of workflow,
workflow or by a human to be audited.

250
00:17:18,123 --> 00:17:20,006
And that's what we do in this audit trail.

251
00:17:20,510 --> 00:17:24,379
We can see which was the governance
rule that got applied, for which

252
00:17:24,381 --> 00:17:27,758
the incident got gated, what were
some of the human decisions that

253
00:17:27,761 --> 00:17:31,287
were taken, whether to approve the
hypothesis or reject the hypothesis.

254
00:17:31,760 --> 00:17:37,317
So-- And AI incident management cannot
be a black box where it can just

255
00:17:37,321 --> 00:17:38,939
go ahead and do what it likes to.

256
00:17:39,447 --> 00:17:43,569
Even that system needs to be kind
of treated as a production system.

257
00:17:44,175 --> 00:17:47,637
So what does the seeded
prototype actually produce?

258
00:17:48,195 --> 00:17:51,487
These are the complete crops
from the seeded beacon prototype

259
00:17:51,490 --> 00:17:52,950
run we just went through.

260
00:17:53,522 --> 00:17:57,796
Notice what the system recommended, a
deployment rollback, not a flag mutation.

261
00:17:58,332 --> 00:18:00,659
This is not a detour from
the feature flag story.

262
00:18:01,118 --> 00:18:02,447
It is the point of the story.

263
00:18:02,991 --> 00:18:06,923
The workflow did not force a
flag rollback simply because

264
00:18:07,118 --> 00:18:08,374
this is a feature flag talk.

265
00:18:08,931 --> 00:18:12,242
The evidence determined the
recommendation, and the flag

266
00:18:12,454 --> 00:18:15,637
was one of the sig-signal
among several other signals.

267
00:18:16,189 --> 00:18:20,045
The artifact shows a scoped chain
set and a pull request-shaped

268
00:18:20,092 --> 00:18:21,541
output after approval.

269
00:18:22,101 --> 00:18:25,093
It demonstrates the governed
handoff in the prototype.

270
00:18:25,528 --> 00:18:29,929
It does not establish production
effectiveness, an MTTR improvement,

271
00:18:30,259 --> 00:18:33,124
or a benchmark result, right?

272
00:18:33,188 --> 00:18:35,894
We, we didn't went and just roll it back.

273
00:18:35,939 --> 00:18:40,752
Yes, we considered that signal that
this could be one of the cause for the

274
00:18:40,752 --> 00:18:42,867
failure, but was not the main cause.

275
00:18:44,108 --> 00:18:49,621
So cohort logic changes with the rollout
stage, like when we compare a ten percent

276
00:18:49,624 --> 00:18:51,209
rollout versus a hundred percent rollout.

277
00:18:51,628 --> 00:18:55,761
So at ten percent rollout, the
cleanest comparison is flagged versus

278
00:18:55,821 --> 00:18:59,803
unflagged traffic in the same window
with equivalent request and user

279
00:18:59,804 --> 00:19:01,411
characteristics wherever possible.

280
00:19:02,785 --> 00:19:07,804
Percentage rollouts are commonly
deterministic for a targeting key, so

281
00:19:07,869 --> 00:19:13,091
we may able to reconstruct, but only if
we preserve the key, the rule, and the

282
00:19:13,093 --> 00:19:20,910
rule set version But at 100% rollout,
the unflagged control no longer exists.

283
00:19:22,048 --> 00:19:27,256
We need to use a pre versus post
comparison with explicit caveats or

284
00:19:27,445 --> 00:19:31,848
request a policy-approved partial
rollback to recreate a bounded control.

285
00:19:32,903 --> 00:19:35,589
Neither design proves cause alone.

286
00:19:36,182 --> 00:19:42,322
At full rollout, the control is gone
So we, we saw a positive scenario,

287
00:19:42,343 --> 00:19:46,684
but what about kind of challenging
the hypothesis before acting, right?

288
00:19:47,435 --> 00:19:51,727
So we need to interpret the
test rather than the intent.

289
00:19:52,259 --> 00:19:57,728
So we need to observe the outcome,
compare the pre versus post, check the

290
00:19:57,728 --> 00:20:02,824
alternatives if it were required, update
the hypothesis as required so we can

291
00:20:02,829 --> 00:20:04,716
preserve the result as new evidence.

292
00:20:06,530 --> 00:20:11,678
If it's a s-- low confidence case, as we
saw in the second one, the cause bundle

293
00:20:11,808 --> 00:20:17,088
remained incomplete, so the system should
not create any rollback package, and

294
00:20:17,088 --> 00:20:19,656
that refusal is a very valid result.

295
00:20:20,036 --> 00:20:24,552
We generally only look for signals
where there's approvals, but even

296
00:20:24,557 --> 00:20:28,657
refusal when there's not even
confidence data is a good signal.

297
00:20:30,044 --> 00:20:33,575
So should we mitigate first
or preserve the experiment?

298
00:20:34,362 --> 00:20:37,239
Sometimes the right answer
is to turn off the flag.

299
00:20:38,047 --> 00:20:42,509
If the customer impact is active,
the change is recent, rollback

300
00:20:42,513 --> 00:20:48,377
is reversible and scoped, and the
likely downside of waiting exceeds

301
00:20:48,377 --> 00:20:51,978
the information of preserving
the rollout, we need to mitigate.

302
00:20:52,978 --> 00:20:57,048
But mitigation and diagnosis
are different objectives.

303
00:20:57,414 --> 00:21:00,679
Yes, when there's a burning
P0, we want mitigation first,

304
00:21:01,068 --> 00:21:02,481
but we also need the diagnosis.

305
00:21:03,481 --> 00:21:07,932
So before changing the exposure, we
need to snapshot the audit event, the

306
00:21:07,932 --> 00:21:12,356
targeting rule, the relevant telemetry,
and record who authorized the change

307
00:21:12,374 --> 00:21:17,826
and why, and then treat recovery or the
absence of recovery as new evidence.

308
00:21:18,611 --> 00:21:20,937
A rollback is also an incident action.

309
00:21:21,586 --> 00:21:25,019
It is not retroactive proof that
the flag caused the incident.

310
00:21:25,748 --> 00:21:28,491
We are doing that because
that's the fastest mitigation

311
00:21:28,496 --> 00:21:29,570
that we can do right now.

312
00:21:30,538 --> 00:21:34,132
Mitigation can be correct
without proving the diagnosis.

313
00:21:34,688 --> 00:21:39,203
But still, we need a control
when it comes to whether it's

314
00:21:39,208 --> 00:21:41,147
mitigation or experimentation.

315
00:21:41,868 --> 00:21:45,368
We need a policy, and as you saw in
the architecture as well as the, in the

316
00:21:45,368 --> 00:21:48,227
demo, policy decides what happens next.

317
00:21:48,956 --> 00:21:52,147
A recommendation to reduce
rollout is only a proposal.

318
00:21:52,953 --> 00:21:56,606
Policy checks the evidence
completeness, reversibility, and scope.

319
00:21:57,261 --> 00:21:58,630
The operator decides.

320
00:21:59,516 --> 00:22:04,114
So even during an active customer
impact, the same chain still applies.

321
00:22:04,701 --> 00:22:09,638
But an incident policy can define
a pa-faster approval path for

322
00:22:09,641 --> 00:22:14,178
a pre-authorized action The
model never owns that change.

323
00:22:14,620 --> 00:22:16,922
Policy decides, operator acts.

324
00:22:17,851 --> 00:22:22,439
So we do not need Beacon to
make flag evidence usable.

325
00:22:23,835 --> 00:22:27,766
We need to start with the, OpenTelemetry
feature flag evaluation vocabulary

326
00:22:28,766 --> 00:22:33,661
and including the feature flag key,
the result value, variant, and the

327
00:22:33,666 --> 00:22:36,276
reason, and the flag set identifier.

328
00:22:38,114 --> 00:22:41,630
Open feature hooks provide lifecycle
points before evaluation, after

329
00:22:41,630 --> 00:22:43,898
evaluation, or error-- or on error.

330
00:22:44,748 --> 00:22:46,176
The practical test is simple.

331
00:22:46,311 --> 00:22:49,358
If you cannot answer who
received the new path, you cannot

332
00:22:49,419 --> 00:22:51,085
compare during the incident.

333
00:22:51,662 --> 00:22:54,293
Where does-- So we talked
about all the happy paths, but

334
00:22:54,297 --> 00:22:55,358
where does this still fail?

335
00:22:56,611 --> 00:22:59,948
The design still fails when the
flag provider has no durable audit

336
00:22:59,956 --> 00:23:04,925
trail, when metadata is missing, when
multiple changes remain correlated,

337
00:23:05,425 --> 00:23:10,567
when stale flags obscure ownership,
or when telemetry cannot identify

338
00:23:10,570 --> 00:23:12,273
whether the flag path was evaluated.

339
00:23:13,298 --> 00:23:15,770
Governance does not
repair missing evidence.

340
00:23:16,238 --> 00:23:21,259
The correct system behavior is to expose
the gap, lower confidence, request

341
00:23:21,280 --> 00:23:26,405
more evidence, and refuse production
mutation when the case is weak.

342
00:23:26,998 --> 00:23:31,736
So I leave with this-- l-leave it
with this, that Beacon's larger

343
00:23:31,750 --> 00:23:34,050
goal is governed incident reasoning.

344
00:23:34,703 --> 00:23:40,201
Feature flags are one important change
source inside that workflow, and they

345
00:23:40,201 --> 00:23:42,271
expose the boundary especially clearly.

346
00:23:43,553 --> 00:23:48,667
I leave it with five rules: preserve
transition and context, challenge

347
00:23:48,702 --> 00:23:55,102
cause with contradictions, separate
observation from mutation, keep policy

348
00:23:55,135 --> 00:23:59,844
outside the model always, and record
change behavior as new evidence.

349
00:24:00,799 --> 00:24:04,000
Feature flag history can
inform the root cause analysis.

350
00:24:04,319 --> 00:24:07,409
Authority still says-- stays explicit.

351
00:24:08,466 --> 00:24:08,713
Thank

352
00:24:08,713 --> 00:24:10,010
you.

